## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** Marker not written.

28 defects remain open (17 critical); `open_defects` correctly FAILs the board.

**Why I deviated from the listed order.** Items 1–7 are all mid-campaign (item 1 is step 5.3 of a 5.x token protocol with three standing blockers). Item 8, `D-PR-FENCE-PREEMPT-WITHOUT-ABORT`, has steps 1–3 DONE and rig-verified with **one** stated closure blocker — the only critical defect within reach of a disposition. Its harness also discharges parts of `D-FENCED-VICTIM-MAY-REREGISTER` step 4 and `D-FENCE-CRASH-MATRIX-UNTESTED`.

**The entry's own step-4 invariant was wrong, and that is the session's main finding.** It demanded "assert the victim's bytes are NOT on the platter." No SCSI target can satisfy that — a bio already handed to the backing device cannot be un-submitted, so as written the defect was unclosable by any implementation. Reading `/src/scst` proved the actual mechanism: `scst_pr_preempt_and_abort()` blocks on `wait_for_completion(pr_aborting_cmpl)` and defers its own SCSI status through `scst_cmd_done_pr_preempt` until every `PR_ABORT_ALL` passes `WAITING_AFFECTED_CMDS_{DONE,FINISHED}`. A held victim write is therefore *expected* to land — strictly **before** the P&A returns. Step 4 now measures and reports two properties separately (cancellation; linearization), and whether "cancellation fails while linearization holds" closes the defect is flagged as its own RULE-5 disposition question — not something to be settled by rewording the entry.

**The RULE-5 consult BLOCKED my test design** with four closure blockers, the sharpest being that `sg_persist` does not exercise the patched MXFS call chain, so it cannot be RULE-6 evidence — plus: a 1-second sleep is not proof the write is in the task set; W=30 s collides with the SCSI command timeout; buffered loop-device reads are unsound across five cache aliases; an SSH-observed process exit is later than the real ioctl return and can misclassify a post-return landing. All seven mandatory corrections and the ruled four-stage plan are in the ledger and ccmemory.

**What I found that makes the expensive stage cheap.** The blocker "prove MXFS's own fence emitted 0x05, not sg_persist's" is answerable from the target itself. SCST `pr` tracing makes clyde's dmesg print, from its own CDB parser, `"Preempt and abort: initiator <iqn>…"` — the substring ` and abort` appears **only** for service action 0x05 — and `"Aborting %d commands for …"`, where N is the victim's task-set size at the instant of the abort, which is exactly the observed in-flight gate the ruling demanded. I enabled it, verified it emits, and **measured its cost at 0 dmesg lines per 20 s at rig idle**, so I left it on to capture the next real fence. Also resolved: READ KEYS shows each key twice (one registrant per path) and `scst_pr_find_registrants_list_key` collects all of them, so one P&A covers both of a node's sessions — the multipath-scope ambiguity does not apply to production, only to purpose-built test LUNs. And the handler-difference blocker is partly refuted in code: `vdisk_file_devtype` and `vdisk_blk_devtype` share `vdisk_task_mgmt_fn_done` and neither implements `task_mgmt_fn_received`, so the abort machinery is entirely SCST core — stage (ii) still runs on fileio, since code agreement is not a measurement.

No source changed; the ledger stays at 28 open and the board criterion still reports the same figure.

